import java.util.*;

class MyStack {
    Queue<Integer> q;

    public MyStack() {
        q = new LinkedList<>();
    }

    public void push(int x) {
        q.add(x);

        int size = q.size();

        for (int i = 0; i < size - 1; i++) {
            q.add(q.remove());
        }
    }

    public int pop() {
        return q.remove();
    }

    public int top() {
        return q.peek();
    }

    public boolean empty() {
        return q.isEmpty();
    }
}
The intuition is to implement a stack (LIFO) using a queue (FIFO). Since a queue normally removes the oldest element first, we rearrange the queue after every push() so that the newly added element comes to the front. In push(), we first add the element using q.add(x), then store the current queue size. The for loop removes each element from the front and adds it back to the rear, except the newly added element, which stays at the front. For example, if the queue is [1,2] and we push 3, it becomes [1,2,3], then we rotate 1 and 2, resulting in [3,1,2]. Therefore, pop() can simply use q.remove() because the most recently added element is already at the front, and top() uses q.peek() to see the top element without removing it. empty() checks whether the queue is empty. The time complexity of push() is O(n) because we may rotate all existing elements, while pop(), top(), and empty() are O(1). The space complexity is O(n) because the queue stores all n elements.
